 ],
 "literal": "The sky's area being covered, spreading, like the kings' / victory-beaten drums, thundering very many times, / the rain does not cease from the sky; the lover / is of a very distant land, and is not before us; / what shall we do, O friend, as the mimosa's / colorful, soft-tufted flowers shed, / and the harsh cold days appear before us?",
 "translation": "Spreading so as to cover the face of the sky, thundering many times like the victory-beaten drums of kings, the clouds do not cease to rain. Our lover is in a very distant land, not here with us. What shall we do, my friend? The harsh, cold days of early winter are appearing before us, making the colorful, soft-tufted flowers of the mimosa bush fall.",
 "modern": "வானம் முழுவதும் மறையும்படிப் பரவி, வெற்றி முரசு கொட்டும் அரசர்களின் முழக்கம் போலப் பலமுறை இடித்து, மேகம் மழையைப் பொழிவதை நிறுத்தவில்லை. நம் காதலர் மிகத் தொலைதூர நாட்டில் இருக்கிறார்; நம்முடன் இல்லை. தோழியே! ஈங்கை மரத்தின் அழகிய மென்மையான பூக்கள் உதிரும்படி, கடுமையான பனிக்காலம் நம் முன்னே வந்துவிட்டதே, நாம் இனி என்ன செய்வோம்?",
 "explanation": "In this Pālai poem, the companion speaks to the heroine, expressing her deep concern over the onset of the cold season while the hero is away. The sky is covered with dark clouds that thunder incessantly like the victory drums of kings, and the rain does not stop. Meanwhile, the hero remains in a very distant land, far from them. The companion points out that the harsh, cold days of early winter are arriving, causing the delicate, colorful, cotton-like flowers of the īṅkai (mimosa) bush to fall. This shedding of flowers serves as a poignant metaphor for the heroine's fading beauty and physical decline due to the pain of separation.",
